>Why do people keep saying that? It is not correct. You are missing the 
>big penalty by ignoring normal effects of killing a monster. When you  
>kill a monster its alignment[1] gets added to your alignment. That is why 
>killing hostile monsters helps your alignment. The case of a coaligned 
>creature generated peaceful their alignment is going to be -9 or lower, 
>and in the case of any peaceful always_peaceful[2] creature their 
>alignment is going to be -15 or lower. 
>
>A few notes:
>[1]By a monsters alignemnt I mean the value of its malign variable as 
>calcualted in mon.c
>[2]Before anyone mentions that "peaceful always_peaceful" is redundant 
>let me point out that creatures that are considered by the game to be 
>always_peaceful can be generated hostile. For example there are hostile 
>aligned priests in planes, but aligned priests are always_peaceful.


